Yes. And I’d make the first version **much smaller than a full Truehost-style platform**.

Your goal for V1 should be:

> **A beautiful Web3Kenya platform where a user can search for an ENS `.eth` name, connect their wallet, register it, and manage its basic records.**

You can expand into other Web3 naming systems, hosting, M-Pesa, etc. **after this works.**

One important current detail: ENS documentation now has ENSv2 material, and it says read-only app integrations largely continue through the same resolution interfaces, while write flows are ENSv2-specific and still evolving. So I'd build your first version with the current ENS architecture in mind rather than hard-coding assumptions about the old system. ([ENS Documentation][1])

