# Your first milestone

Don't think:

> "I need to build a domain registrar."

Think:

> **"I need to build a Web3Kenya interface that can successfully register one ENS name from a user's wallet."**

Once you can do this:

```text
                    USER
                      │
                      ▼
               web3kenya domains
                      │
                Search "xyz"
                      │
                      ▼
                xyz.eth ✓
                      │
                 Connect Wallet
                      │
                      ▼
                  Register
                      │
                      ▼
                 MetaMask
                      │
                      ▼
                 Ethereum
                      │
                      ▼
                  ENS
                      │
                      ▼
               xyz.eth owned
```

**you have built the core of the product.**

Everything else—beautiful dashboards, M-Pesa, multiple naming protocols, decentralized hosting, analytics, reseller accounts, APIs, subdomains, etc.—can be layered on afterward.

### Your immediate study order

I'd personally have **you** go:

**TypeScript → viem → wagmi → ENS resolution → ENS records → ENS registration → Supabase → SIWE → testing → production**

And because you're already doing **Solidity, Next.js, Vercel and Supabase**, you're actually much closer to this than it might seem.

For the ENS portion, bookmark the official **[ENS developer documentation](https://docs.ens.domains/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)**—especially its resolution, resolver, registration and ENSv2 sections. ([ENS Documentation][9])

If we were doing this together, **I would make Project 1 your starting point rather than having you spend weeks studying theory**: build a tiny `Web3Kenya Domains` prototype that connects a wallet and resolves an `.eth` name, then progressively turn that prototype into the actual platform.

